  |title The discount function and the unquestioning are one operation

  |summary Deciding a document is not worth reading and deciding a premise is
    settled are the same computation — *does this let me proceed* — run before
    evaluation rather than by it.

  |formal-expression
    Two behaviours that present as separate failures are one operation with two
    objects. A document that would slow the current line down is classified
    low-value; a premise the current line requires is classified settled.
    Neither classification is an evaluation. Both arrive *before* evaluation and
    occupy its seat, which is why the discount is experienced as judgment: it is
    judgment-shaped and judgment-timed, and not judgment-caused.

    The classification is anti-correlated with value by construction rather than
    by accident. What most needs attention is what would change the plan — a
    long file, a stale-labelled file, an original that would have to be
    reconciled against work already done. Each is expensive *because* it is
    load-bearing. So cheapness-to-proceed and actual value point in opposite
    directions, reliably.

    The operational consequence is that the bias is usable as an instrument when
    read backwards. *This is obviously low-value* is a positive signal, not a
    conclusion; *that is settled* is suspect exactly when it became settled at
    the moment it was needed.

  |epistemic-status
    Derived from a structural argument about ordering — the classification
    precedes evaluation — with three same-day specimens in which every item of
    value recovered had been priced low beforehand ( #exm-priced-low-three-of-three,
    unwritten). One day, one observer, and the observer was the subject.

    Max attainable: **empirical**. The experiment that would raise it: record
    skip-decisions with their felt-obviousness at the moment of skipping, then
    score later against what the skipped material turned out to contain. Until
    something like that runs, the anti-correlation is argued rather than
    measured.

    Load-bearing: the claim that the classification runs *before* evaluation. If
    it were a bad evaluation rather than a pre-emption of one, the repair would
    be "evaluate better," and the observed data — that the skip never presents
    as a decision — would be unexplained.

  |discussion
    The two-object framing is what makes this a mechanism rather than two
    observations. It also explains why interventions aimed at either symptom
    separately have not worked: *read more thoroughly* and *check your premises*
    are two instructions to the same unexamined step.

    The practical descendants are #prx-what-did-i-decide-not-to-read and
    #prx-untruncated-primary. The first targets the document object, the second
    targets the moment the truncation is typed — which is a different moment,
    and the separation is deliberate.

  |working-notes
    Joseph's form of the same observation, from the other direction and stated
    before this one: *"the stunning frequency of things unquestioned once
    'actionable' … probably not a single mechanical action on this repository
    doesn't have some form of some of this error infecting it."* The convergence
    is one mind and one agent in dialogue, not independent corroboration.

    Open: whether the *deprecation banner is a deadline, not a discount* case is
    a corollary of this or a separate mechanism. Currently written as a special
    case in the source, and it may earn its own segment.
